I'm at the University of Sydney where I  studied and received my Bachelor of  Education degree I'm helping out with a  program called compass which is run by  the University for high school students  and you can read more details about it  in at their website if you're curious  it's been a strange mix of feelings  being on campus though I haven't been  back to uni since I graduated there's a  part of me that feels really at home  here  walking among the buildings that make  you feel a bit like you're at Hogwarts  I suppose that isn't surprising better I  spent four years of my life here and I  always came to uni five days a week but  there's another part of me that feels  very disoriented and confused being here  and the campus itself looks very  different now there was when I was a  student and walking from railway Square  to Victoria Park  I noticed quite a few old buildings been  torn down new ones had either replaced  them or were in the midst of  construction crossing the footbridge  from Wentworth over City Road is bizarre  because it goes in a different direction  now than before and the fancy law  building on Eastern Avenue it was  literally just a huge hole in the ground  during my final year of study but it's  not mainly the physical aspects of the  university that are giving me this  cognitive disconnect I walked past the  front lawn just now and saw a bunch of  students lounging around on the grass  and I figure they must be on a two or  three hour break or something like that  back in the day I had quite a few of  those because I attended math lectures  which traditionally were held at 8:00  a.m. and finished at 9:00 and then I'd  have to wait around till 11 or 12 when  my first education class was on so I  remember somewhere in the back of my  mind what it was like to have breaks  like that but now that I've been a  full-time worker and also a parent for a  number of years now it just seems weird  for me to look at people sitting around  doing nothing for such long stretches of  time seeing those students brought back  a lot of memories but also let me think  of a few things I wanted to say so here  from this perspective is someone young  enough to remember uni but old enough to  see it with some objectivity are three  thoughts on University students and time  firstly unis students use your time  wisely this is a very special and  limited chapter of life so you don't  want to waste it I don't want to  caricature or unisons as lazy people I  remember my time at university has been  a busy they were definitely way more  full than my days at high school but the  plain fact of the matter is no one the  possible exception of retirees has the  sheer quantity of disposable time that  unist in tap now most you knees have two  semesters that are 13 weeks long which  is 26 weeks where you're obliged to turn  to classes and sit assessments and so on  that means you have 50% of the year  where you can literally do whatever you  like now I know that's something of an  exaggeration because people have  responsibilities to part-time jobs and  other things like that but it's not that  far from the truth so the question for  you is what will you do with all those  hundreds of hours will you sleep study  work travel read learn an instrument  party volunteer hang with friends  whatever you do make sure it's something  you can look back at from the vantage  point of someone working 9:00 to 5:00  every day with just four weeks of annual  leave year from respective of someone  who no longer has that same kind of  disposable time make sure you can look  back from that perspective and say I'm  glad I made those choices in other words  spend your time wisely by the way please  don't get me wrong when you enter  full-time work it's not like they lock  you in a prison or something workers  have a very different kind of freedom to  uni students but that's a topic for  another day and another video so how do  you know if you're spending your time  wisely or not well that leads me to the  second thing I wanted to say which is  uni students spend your time  purposefully think long and hard and  work out what the purpose of your time  at university is and the most obvious  answer that question is it's a stepping  stone to full-time work or fair enough  but there's so much more to unit and  vocational training and to be honest in  most jobs these days including mine the  lion's share of what you need to know  about work will be learned at work not  unit basically you need to be able to  answer the question what am I going to  this place for why spend three or four  or five or more years doing University  what's the purpose that's going to make  all those hundreds of hours traveling  and those thousands of dollars in debt  all worthwhile is it to please your  parents form new relationships challenge  your own view  of the world once you establish that  then you can decide the ways to spend  your time that will be in accordance  with those goals so University students  spend your time wisely spend your time  purposefully and lastly spend your time  thankfully it's a truism of human  experience but no matter what we're  doing our hearts easily gravitate toward  and facile on the hard and frustrating  things in our present situation no  matter what that situation might be  that's what us humans are like it takes  effort to be grateful for things but  whinging and complaining come naturally  to us it doesn't matter how good your  circumstances are or how long you've  been looking forward to them  it only takes a very short amount of  time to discover and dwell on the things  that annoy you I mean some of us spent  our entire HSC year looking forward to  its end and anticipating University and  everything that will be awesome about it  and then we got there and we're all like  oh my timetable is awful the coffee is  so expensive you know my lecture makes  me sleepy I had to pull an all-nighter  because I was finishing those four  assignments that were all due at the  same time and I hate listening too noisy  high school students on public transport  so that's always say and that's how we  feel and we let the negatives dominate  our experience but this is just classic  firstworldproblems you know there are  people in the world who would die for a  primary education and here we are  complaining about receiving a tertiary  education so just stop and remember what  a privilege it is to be able to go to  university remember all the benefits  that we take for granted and that can  only come up come through those with  university experiences and  qualifications remember that you live in  a society that recognizes the worth of  educating its young adults rather than  putting them straight into the unskilled  workforce and is therefore willing to  loan you any students billions of  taxpayer dollars in order to make  widespread higher education a viable  alternative even to those without a cent  to be a name remember it and be grateful  for it spend your time wisely spend your  time purposefully and spend your time  thankfully  went regret it
 

